## bench/vm-perms.sh   (nixgen-perms.service, in-box over serial)

Boot-time metadata replay, per manifest class, ms. Anchor: systemd
charged the service 53.3 ms on the ISO boot, staged replay of the
equivalent committed base 49-59 ms, so staging models the boot.

class    rows      before    after     per row after
mode     20k/100k  284/1137  268/1232  ~12 us    (batched chmod)
own      20k/100k  20899/143974  321/1395  ~13 us (batched chown)
caps     5k        5738      1908      ~380 us   (one exec per row)
acls     20k files 222       237       ~1 us     (setfacl --restore)
xattrs   20k files 199       206       ~3 us     (setfattr --restore)
attrs    5k        4526      56        0         (bails at row 1)

Size is not the variable, exec count is. What the sweep was built to
find: 100k user-owned files (a committed /home, which is what an
install without nixdata= produces) cost 144 s of chown forks before
sysinit.target, every boot. Grouping by owner pair, the way the mode
loop already grouped by mode, put it next to the mode number.

Two caveats on the before column. It was measured on the pre-REBUILD
ISO, and fork-heavy loops ran ~3x slower there for reasons outside
this change: caps is untouched code and moved 5738 -> 1908 across the
rebuild alone. Discount the own win to ~35x rather than 103x. And
attrs' before is 2409 ms on the new ISO (the first fix probed a
synthetic file, which overlayfs accepts because a freshly created path
has no lower to copy up from; every real row still failed).

Nothing here is worth batching further: caps rows are
capability-carrying binaries, 2 on a stock tree, and setcap has no
one-caps-many-files form.

## syscall profile   (import-dir of arch-base, strace -c -f / perf stat)

Import is three-way overlapped: the coroutine spine (dumpPath reads +
NAR framing, restore writes) plus AsyncHashSink (whole-NAR digest) and
AsyncFileHasher (per-file digests, streaming dedup), each on its own
thread behind a 64-slot queue. Roughly half the CPU is kernel time, so
syscall counts are the thing to watch, not just wall clock.

                    original   buffered+filter   +canonical restore   +edge wake
futex                939,993      415,188            ~436,000            385,384
newfstatat           188,153      155,072             121,991            121,995
llistxattr            33,082       33,082                   1                  1
chmod (path)          25,416       25,416               1,811              1,811
geteuid               33,085       33,085                   3                  3
getpid                     -            -               9,738                  0
task-clock            2116 ms     ~1580 ms            ~1570 ms           ~1570 ms
wall                  1.31 s      ~1.02 s              0.97 s             0.97 s

sourceToSink was unbuffered while its mirror image sinkToSource has
always wrapped its side in a 32 KiB CoroBufferedSink, with a comment
saying why. dumpPath emits the NAR as tiny tokens ("(", "type",
"regular", length words, padding), so unbuffered each token resumed
the coroutine and became its own queue push into both hash threads: a
mutex, a notify and an allocation apiece. Upstream mostly uses the
buffered direction; import-dir uses the other one, on every commit.

Follow-ups tried, with verdicts (measure, then promote or discard):

- PROMOTED: the source-tree stat doubling was import-dir's noSpecials
  PathFilter: archive.cc called filter(path) (a raw ::lstat) and then
  immediately dump() -> accessor.lstat(path), while readDirectory had
  already returned every entry's type from d_type. dumpPath now skips
  fifo/socket entries itself and import-dir passes no filter: 188k ->
  155k newfstatat, one lstat per entry. Wall-neutral on NVMe (hot
  dentry cache); the win is code shape + sys time. Behavior pinned by
  tests/specials-skip.sh, since the old mechanism is gone.
- DISCARDED: memoising tryDedup's farm probes (hash -> size, worker
  thread owns the map). A/B on the warm all-dup import: 107,571 vs
  104,459 newfstatat, 3% for 17 lines. The 440x-probed .links entries
  in the profile belong to the optimise pass (one probe per file
  sharing that content), not tryDedup; optimise with capture is
  already 0.2 s and parallel, so not worth chasing either.
- DISCARDED: raising SourceToSink's BufferedSink from 32 KiB to
  256 KiB. ~5% wall, futex 425k -> 385k: a knob, not a fix, and the
  remaining futexes are AsyncFileHasher's per-file metadata events
  (~4 per file), not data chunks. Batching those events is the one
  real cut left on the futex side, if it is ever worth the churn.
  Re-swept after the canonical restore landed: 8K/32K/256K measure
  463k/446k/446k futex and identical wall, so above the 32 KiB
  default the curve is flat and the knob is settled, not just
  discarded. Alignment is a non-factor throughout: no O_DIRECT, and
  the hashers take unaligned input.

- PROMOTED: canonical restore (upstream's own FIXME at the old
  canonicalise call site: "merge into restorePath"). RestoreSink in
  canonical mode creates files 0444, stamps mtime 1 through the still
  open fd, fchmods exec files 0555 without the fstat, canonicalises
  each directory when its subtree completes, and symlink mtimes go
  through the parent fd. The post-restore canonicalisePathMetaData
  walk disappears: llistxattr 33k -> 1, geteuid 33k -> 3, path chmod
  25.4k -> 1.8k, newfstatat 155k -> 122k. On nixgen-commit's store
  disk that is one journaled metadata write less per restored file,
  which is where USB-class media pays 1-2 ms apiece. Two ordering
  traps, both hit and fixed: directory canonicalisation must wait for
  the streamed dedup (its link+rename fails against 0555 and bumps the
  fresh mtime), so capture-mode restores defer dirs until the hasher
  drains; and the restore root must stay writable until moveFile has
  renamed it out of the temp dir (cross-parent directory renames need
  write on the moved dir), so the sink never finishes the root and the
  final-path owner runs canonicaliseTimestampAndPermissions once.
  moveFile silently swallows non-EXDEV rename failures, which turned
  both traps into "path vanished" mysteries two calls later.
  Fallbacks stay on the old full walk: Flat restores, and a store dir
  carrying a default POSIX ACL (children would inherit xattrs the walk
  used to strip; one llistxattr on the store dir per import decides).
  Verified: store-path hashes byte-identical, all 58 unit TAP ok,
  arch meta-test PASS (savemeta/restmeta contract bit-identical),
  update-test FAST=1 full PASS (commit/update/remove/switch/setup in
  the box, three boots). Also retired bootstrap.sh's destructive
  find -delete of sockets/fifos (the dump skips them itself).

- DISCARDED: dropping loadInodeHash() from the per-path optimise (the
  farm scan reads the whole .links dir per import, which smelled like
  O(store) paid per commit). Measured on the warm all-dup import: the
  scan is ~100 batched getdents that lets readDirectoryIgnoringInodes
  drop already-linked files from the walk entirely; without it the
  walk pays 2 extra lstats per deduped file. 74,490 newfstatat with
  the scan vs 121,662 without: the shortcut wins until a farm of
  ~14M entries, which no box will see. The one scenario that could
  flip it (cold farm directory on USB-class flash vs cache-hot
  lstats) needs a real box to measure; on syscall count it loses
  everywhere.

- DISCARDED: -march=native (measured, not just reasoned). Three runs
  each: 1480-1496 ms task-clock native vs 1467-1502 generic, synth
  populate 0.886 vs 0.883 s. The hot loops never see compile flags:
  SHA-256 (libcrypto) and glibc memcpy both dispatch SHA-NI/AVX2 by
  CPUID at runtime, half the time is kernel-side anyway, and
  native-built payload binaries would SIGILL on older boxes the ISO
  boots. If a CPU floor is ever wanted, -march=x86-64-v3 is the
  shippable form of the same idea; expect the same nothing.

- PROMOTED: parallel single-path optimise. optimiseStore fans out one
  task per store path, so a commit -- which optimises exactly the path
  it just imported -- had one task and ran the whole walk serially.
  optimisePath_ now takes the pool and gives each child directory its
  own task; the writable-toggle stays single-writer by construction,
  since a frame's own file children never leave it and only subtrees
  fork. A directory's restore need not wait on those tasks: a file
  needs its immediate parent writable, nothing above. d_type off
  readdir picks dir-vs-file free (DT_UNKNOWN recurses inline, the old
  path). Stats are atomics on a shared context, not per-task structs
  merged under a mutex. Interleaved: optimise 0.567 -> 0.225 s capture
  off, 0.228 -> 0.154 s on, link counts identical. Scaling 0.235 /
  0.204 / 0.170 / 0.156 s at 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 CPUs, flat past 8: the
  floor is the journal. Four assertions added to parallel-optimise.cc
  on the single-path API, plus the canonical-mode sweep now covers it.

- PROMOTED: wake the async hash queues only on the empty -> non-empty
  edge (producer only when a pop clears a full ring). Both are SPSC and
  the consumer only sleeps after seeing an empty queue under the mutex,
  so a notify per push was a futex wake with nobody listening. futex
  420,093 -> 385,384, import 1.02x. Only 8% because the elided wakes
  were never most of them: the hashers keep up, so the queue is empty
  at push time and the edge fires anyway. What remains is real
  blocking. Retires "batching those events is the one real cut left".

- PROMOTED (free): hoist getpid() out of the temp-link name. glibc
  dropped its pid cache in 2.25, so it was a syscall per linked file,
  9,738 of them. Not wall-visible; just had no reason to be in a loop.

- DISCARDED: temp link in the target file's own directory rather than
  the store root, on the theory that parallel threads contend on the
  one directory. Measured 0.163-0.171 s against 0.152-0.174 s: nothing,
  which also says the post-8-CPU plateau is the journal, not that
  directory. It would also turn a crash between link and rename from
  inert junk in the store root into a .tmp-link-* inside a store path,
  breaking that path's hash.

- PROMOTED, after first being discarded on host numbers: replace
  sourceToSink's coroutine with a real thread (bounded ring of recycled
  buffers). A coroutine is not a thread -- producer and consumer share
  one stack -- so source reads and destination writes never overlap.
  On the host that measured 1.12x and was discarded. In the box it is
  1.50x on the import phase (2.7 -> 1.8 s) and 1.33x on a whole commit
  (3990 -> 3009 ms), three runs each, no overlap between the two sets.
  The host was wrong about the *source medium*: it dumps a tree sitting
  in page cache, where reads are memcpy and the producer is 18% of the
  spine. A box reads its root off compressed squashfs, where reads cost
  real CPU, and serialising that decompression against the writes is
  most of what the coroutine was costing. Any host bench that warms the
  source tree first will understate this permanently.

Still open, measured: ~75k target-store stats remain (optimise walk +
farm probes); optimise with capture is 0.15 s and parallel, so the
remaining spend is known and cheap.

Import on the host is near its floor: dumpPath alone is 0.106 s of a
0.58 s import, the whole-NAR SHA-256 is 0.231 s already on its own
thread, and a plain `cp -a` of the tree costs 0.33 s -- about 1.8x a
raw copy while also computing two SHA-256 digests over 552 MiB and
canonicalising every file. It scales 1.14 -> 0.63 s from 1 to 2 CPUs
and is flat past 4. Beating it means less I/O or less hashing, not
more threads. What the host cannot see is the source medium, which is
what the in-box numbers above turned on.

## bench/run-large.sh   (driver-heavy generations, i5-14600KF, NVMe ext4)

Every shape above varies file *count*. A generation carrying firmware,
kernel modules and gpu blobs varies file *size* instead: two orders of
magnitude fewer files per gigabyte, so per-file costs stop mattering
and per-byte ones take over. bench-large holds the total constant and
sweeps size to tell one from the other; bench-commit puts a sync()
between import and optimise so their costs are separable; and
trace-import timestamps import-dir's counter repaints, because the
symptom users report is not a total, it is the number freezing.

bench-large, 1024 MiB total, warm, file size 1 / 8 / 64 / 256 MiB:

  dump->null            16417 / 17935 / 13457 / 15651 MiB/s
  dump->sha256           2078 /  2088 /  2090 /  2092 MiB/s
  dump->s2s->null       10609 / 10881 / 11572 / 12033 MiB/s
  dump->s2s->restore     5421 /  5616 /  5397 /  5123 MiB/s

Flat to the noise across 256x in file size: nothing in libutil's byte
path is size-dependent. The per-byte ceiling is the digest, 2.1 GiB/s,
which is what `openssl speed sha256` reports on this box (SHA-NI), and
the two digests an import runs sit on their own threads, so the
pipeline ceiling is one core's SHA-256, not two.

Real tree (arch-base + /usr/lib/{firmware,modules,gcc} + every >20 MiB
.so on a desktop: nvidia, LLVM, Qt WebEngine -- 3318 MiB, 50k entries):

  dump->null       0.361 s   9192 MiB/s
  dump->sha256     1.709 s   1942 MiB/s
  dump->s2s->null  0.451 s   7364 MiB/s
  dump->s2s->restore 2.037 s 1630 MiB/s
  flush (sync)     2.607 s   1273 MiB/s

Note the last line is not free and is not counted anywhere else: the
restore returns with the whole generation dirty in page cache.

## why a large commit chokes, and where the cost actually is

The choke is not in the code path. Same tree, same binary, the only
variable being how much page cache the importer is allowed:

  cap        import   worst counter gap   import spent stalled
  none        2.25 s        0.16 s              0%
  2 GiB      10.55 s        8.33 s             79%   (one freeze)
  1 GiB      12.67 s        4.30 s             71%   (three)

(systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=, which is what a box
committing under a tmpfs overlay upper looks like; unconstrained on a
31 GiB host the dirty limit is never reached and the effect does not
exist.) The 8.33 s freeze completes 24 files: the pipeline is blocked
in write() under balance_dirty_pages, not doing per-file work. That is
exactly the reported symptom -- "it slows at certain parts" -- and it
lands on the big files, because they are what carries the dirty pool
past the limit.

Unconstrained the bill still arrives, just later and out of sight:
import 2.4 s then a 3.1-8.5 s sync, and optimise measured 0.559 s
against 0.226 s with a sync() barrier in front of it -- 2.5x, purely
its journaled metadata writes queueing behind the import's writeback.
Any number here that does not include a trailing sync is an advance
against the next phase.

Two consequences for reading the rest of this file. Host imports are
measured with gigabytes of spare page cache, so they never show the
stall; and streamed dedup's "never hit disk" holds only while the file
is small enough that writeback has not started on it. A 1802 MiB
generation reported 812 MiB deduped -- 45% of the write pressure was
content already in the store, written and then discarded.

- DISCARDED: write-behind in RestoreRegularFile (sync_file_range
  SYNC_FILE_RANGE_WRITE every N MiB of a file, so writeback drains
  steadily instead of accumulating). Import+sync totals, one run each:

    cap none   off 5.5 s   4 MiB 12.0 s   32 MiB 14.8 s
    cap 1 GiB  off 14.2 s  4 MiB 11.2 s   32 MiB 15.4 s

  It moves the stall around rather than removing it: with cache
  headroom it is a 2x regression (forcing early writeback in small
  batches defeats ext4 delayed allocation, so blocks get allocated and
  journalled piecemeal), and under the cap it buys ~20% while the
  import still takes 9.4 s. Max counter gap did fall 8.33 -> 2.56 s,
  so the shape improves and the total does not. Not shippable as an
  unconditional constant, and a knob nobody can reach from arch/ is
  worth less than the 20 lines. Reverted.

Still open, unmeasured: the only change that removes work rather than
rescheduling it is not writing dedupable bytes at all, and a NAR gives
no per-file hash before its contents, so nothing can probe the farm
early. Bounded buffering would cover small files, which are already
cheap, and never the blobs, which are the ones that stall.

## fixes landed from the driver-generation audit

Measured on the same box. Host tests: 86/86 TAP ok, arch meta-test PASS
(gen A and gen B produced identical NAR hashes, which is the no-op
rebuild contract). ISO-dependent tests not run.

- PROMOTED: capture symlink digests at import. optimisePath excluded
  symlinks from the captured-hash map, so each one fell through to
  hashPath(), which opens it O_NOFOLLOW, takes the ELOOP, reopens the
  parent by full absolute path and readlinks: three syscalls and two
  allocations for a digest the restore already had the target for. A
  store tree is roughly a quarter symlinks. On arch-base (33,082
  entries, 8,118 symlinks) readlinkat went 2 per symlink to exactly 1
  (8,118 calls) and openat errors 8,118 -> 14, about 24k syscalls gone.
  Framing is hand-written in AsyncFileHasher::symlink and pinned by
  tests/import-hashes.cc, which rehashes every captured key from disk
  and now covers a short target, a 200-byte one, a dangling one and two
  sharing a target. ctx.total stopped adding `symlinks` separately,
  since `files` now holds them.

- FIXED (memory safety): SourceToSink::pull was not idempotent at EOF.
  `consumed = 0` sat after the throw, so the recycle above left
  consuming empty while consumed held the last chunk's length; a second
  read past EndOfFile skipped the refill block and computed
  size() - consumed as a size_t underflow, memcpying ~2^64 bytes from a
  moved-from buffer. Reading a Source past EOF is something ChainSource
  and drainInto both do by design, and the coroutine this replaced
  re-threw every time, so the thread rewrite introduced it. Reachable
  via addToStoreFromDump when the tree fits narBufferSize and the
  ingestion methods differ; no in-tree caller hits that today.

- FIXED (race): tryDedup built its temp link as "<file>.dedup~" INSIDE
  the store path being restored. A tree containing both X and X.dedup~
  put the hasher thread's link() against the restore thread's
  O_CREAT|O_EXCL, and whichever lost aborted the import. It also left
  junk inside a store path if we died between link and rename, which
  breaks that path's hash. This file already discarded exactly that
  layout for optimise's temp link, for the second reason. Now
  <store>/.tmp-dedup-<pid>-<n>, same shape as optimisePath_. The
  cleanup unlink's return is checked too: the rename failure it follows
  is the mode/mtime ordering trap already documented above, so a silent
  leftover there is a path registered under a content address that no
  longer describes it.

- FIXED (ordering): hasher.fileEnd() was queued from inside
  RestoreSink's callback, but RestoreSink flushes and stamps the file
  after the callback returns, so streamed dedup could link+rename over
  a file whose tail was still in the FdSink buffer. Survivable only
  because everything after func() is fd-relative and landed on the
  orphaned inode. fileEnd now runs after createRegularFile returns, so
  tryDedup's "the file just restored is complete" is true rather than
  incidental.

- FIXED: openat2 treated only ENOSYS as "unsupported". A seccomp filter
  predating openat2 returns EPERM instead, which Docker's default
  profile did for years and this repo ships a Dockerfile; under one,
  every open in the restore path failed instead of falling back to the
  iterative resolver that exists for it. EPERM and EOPNOTSUPP now cache
  the same way.

- REMOVED: sinkToSource, which had zero callers. With it go
  boost::coroutine2, protected_fixedsize_stack,
  defaultCoroutineStackSize, the boost::context forward declaration in
  serialise.hh, the ['context'] module in libutil's meson.build, and the
  libboost_context link. Boost is now header-only across the whole
  fork and libnixutil's only non-toolchain dependency is libcrypto.
  tests/needed-drift.sh pins the new NEEDED set.

- CHANGED: SourceToSink's `full` ring is a deque. As a vector, every
  pop was an erase(begin()), an O(queue) move of the remaining chunk
  handles. free_ stays a vector: any spare buffer will do, so it is a
  LIFO.

- PROMOTED: nixgen-savemeta ran six find walks and four full-path sorts
  over one entry set. The four probe manifests (caps, acls, xattrs,
  attrs) now share one traversal and two sorts; the type is stripped
  before sorting, because sorting the typed record would order by type
  first and reshuffle every manifest. Verified byte-identical on a
  synthetic tree (spaces, unicode, leading-dash names, an ACL, an
  xattr), on arch-base (33,083 perms rows), and through the meta-test
  round-trip. 0.372 -> 0.295 s min of 3 on 33k entries warm: less than
  the walk-count suggests, because the per-path probes are most of the
  cost and they are irreducible (no bulk form of getcap/getfacl/
  getfattr/lsattr). The saving is the three traversals and two sorts.

- PROMOTED (free): nixgen-commit's `du -sxm "$SNAP"` was a full stat
  walk of the whole snapshot to print an advisory note the script
  ignored either way, computing an upper bound so far above reality
  (the import dedups against the farm as it streams) that it warned on
  stores with ample room. Replaced with the df figure it already had.

- KEPT, measured neutral: AsyncHashSink deriving from BufferedSink. It
  is fed by the TeeSource the *restore* reads through, and parseDump
  reads a NAR the way it is framed, so unbuffered it took 1,740,668
  pushes averaging 2005 bytes on a 3.3 GiB tree (48 per file) against
  193,620 at 18 KB on the per-file hasher beside it. Buffering takes
  that to ~158k, an 11x cut in queue pushes and heap allocations.
  Hashes byte-identical. Wall clock and CPU time both unmoved,
  interleaved 5 pairs at 20 CPUs and under taskset -c 0-1 -- which is
  the expected result for removing work that was never on the critical
  path, not a failure. The dump direction got this fix when
  sourceToSink learned to buffer; the restore direction never did.

Note on futex counts anywhere in this file: strace -f inserts a ptrace
stop per syscall, which changes thread interleaving and manufactures
contention. The 945k -> 462k this change showed is directional only.
Allocation and push counts, derived from the code, are the honest
metric.

- KEPT, host cannot measure it: drop a deduped file's dirty pages
  instead of writing them. tryDedup renames a farm hard link over the
  file it just wrote, which unlinks the copy but leaves its dirty pages
  queued; ftruncate through a descriptor borrowed from the restore
  discards them, and on ext4 most are still delayed allocation so the
  reservation is released without a block ever being allocated. The
  descriptor has to be duplicated inside RestoreSink's callback and
  carried on the End event: store files are created 0444, only the
  creating descriptor has write access, and after the rename the inode
  has no name to reopen by. Verified firing: on a 514 MiB tree of 8x64
  MiB blobs plus 300 small files, exactly 8 dup and 8 ftruncate(fd, 0),
  matching the files over the 1 MiB threshold.
  Measured effect on this box: none, and the reason is instructive.
  The all-dup re-commit takes 0.25 s for 513 MiB, so the window between
  writing a blob and the hasher deduping it is milliseconds and
  writeback never starts; the unlink alone drops the pages. Device
  sectors across the import (/sys/block/nvme0n1/stat) measured 3-39 MiB
  written either way, under a 512 MiB cgroup cap, noise swamping any
  signal. So this is the sourceToSink situation again: the mechanism
  only pays where writeback actually fires during the import, which is
  a box committing 1.8 GiB in 6.1 s off slower media, not a host doing
  513 MiB in 0.25 s off NVMe. Cost if it never pays: two syscalls per
  deduped file above 1 MiB (8 on that tree). Below the threshold it
  does nothing at all, because a file written and unlinked within
  milliseconds never reached writeback and the three syscalls would buy
  nothing. Wants an in-box A/B before it can be called a win.

- CHANGED: addToStoreFromDump's narBufferSize prefill grows
  geometrically. A rootfs NAR always runs it to the full 32 MiB, and
  stepping there 64 KiB at a time took ~512 reallocs of a buffer that
  spends most of its life above the mmap threshold; doubling gets there
  in ~9. Behaviour identical, inMemory still decided by whether the
  source ends first.

- NOT DONE, and recorded so it is not reconsidered: the double
  isValidPath / isValidPathUncached at addToStoreFromDump. Two SQLite
  queries, but once per import, not per file. The first exists to avoid
  taking PathLocks and is doing its job.

## boost: audited, and the answer is nowhere

Two independent audits (one linear, one a 13-agent fan-out over 97
inventoried sites) reached the same verdict on the four hand-rolled
concurrency sites: SourceToSink, AsyncHashSink, AsyncFileHasher and
ThreadPool should NOT use a boost primitive. Reasons, so this does not
get relitigated:

- boost::lockfree::spsc_queue has no rvalue push and no move-out pop.
  It copy-constructs in and copy-assigns out, where the current code
  moves and SourceToSink additionally recycles the allocation. On a
  byte-bound workload that ADDS a memcpy each way.
- It is wait-free but non-blocking: push fails when full, pop fails
  when empty. Both rings here are bounded by design and genuinely hit
  both ends, so full/empty needs a condvar, which needs a mutex. The
  result is mutex + 2 condvars + a lock-free ring + a free-index ring:
  strictly more machinery to save one uncontended lock cmpxchg pair
  (~20 ns) per chunk, against ~30 us of SHA-256 for that same chunk.
- boost::lockfree::queue rejects the Ev type outright (non-trivial
  destructor).
- boost::fibers::buffered_channel is a semantic match but blocks the
  fiber, not the thread; with plain std::threads and no other fibers
  its push-on-full degenerates to a yield loop, worse than a futex wait
  for a producer that blocks for milliseconds under writeback
  throttling. It also needs libboost_fiber.
- boost::iostreams::stream_buffer in place of BufferedSink is a strict
  regression: the value of BufferedSink here is the oversized-chunk
  bypass, and neither libstdc++'s xsputn nor boost's indirect_streambuf
  overrides it. Today 0 of 3.3 GiB passes through those buffers; under
  a streambuf it would be 3.3 GiB times four instances.
- boost::hash2::sha2_256 has no SHA-NI path, 5-8x slower than the
  libcrypto call at hash.cc:310.

Where the fork already uses boost (concurrent_flat_set for InodeHash,
unordered_flat_map in gc, container::small_vector) those are cases with
no std equivalent and a real algorithmic win. The queues are not.

The structural reason none of it matters: one SHA-256 pass over a 3.3
GiB tree is 1.709 s of a ~2.2 s import, the two passes already run
concurrently on two threads, and SHA-256 is not parallelisable within a
stream. Four threads buy 1.7x total (5.74 / 4.37 / 3.62 / 3.40 / 3.32 s
at 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 20 CPUs). Fanning the per-file digests across a pool
would drop that term below the whole-NAR pass and leave the floor
exactly where it is.

Two non-boost leads came out of the sweep, both unmeasured:
- StringSource and TeeSource have no drainInto override, so the verify
  path in arch/import-path.cc bounces every byte through a 64 KiB stack
  array in Source::drainInto. Overrides would delete one whole-NAR
  memcpy. That is `nixgen import`, not nixgen-commit.
- Source::skip uses an 8 KiB buffer and is the content path for
  import-path's first pass (NullFileSystemObjectSink sets skipContents).

## the stutter, caught: two write() calls, 6.4 s

`strace -f -T -e trace=write` on the 3.3 GiB driver tree, same binary,
same tree, only the page cache the importer may use varies:

  unconstrained   84,085 writes, 0.84 s in write(), max   0.001 s
  MEMMAX=1G       84,085 writes, 10.69 s in write(), max  3.224 s

  bucket        <1ms   1-10ms  10-100ms  0.1-1s  >1s
  unconstrained 84082       3         0       0    0
  MEMMAX=1G     83347     728         7       1    2

Import wall 5.0 s -> 17.0 s, and the extra 12 s is almost exactly the
extra time spent inside write() (10.69 - 0.84 = 9.85 s). The write
*count* is identical, so this is not more work; it is the same work
parked. 99.1% of writes still return in under a millisecond. **Two
writes take 3.22 s each**, and those two are 6.4 s of the 17.

That is the whole reported symptom. The restore thread sits inside one
write() in balance_dirty_pages; the progress counter is driven from
AsyncFileHasher's per-file end(), which starves the moment the restore
thread stops feeding the event queue, so the number freezes for three
seconds and then jumps. Nothing in the import is slow. The kernel is
holding the writer still because the box has no page cache to spare.

Consequences for anything measured here: a host with free RAM cannot
see this at all (max write 1 ms), and no amount of queue, lock or hash
work moves it. The only levers are fewer bytes written, or making
writeback engage early and smoothly instead of letting the dirty pool
build until the throttle has to stall hard. The second is a
vm.dirty_bytes / vm.dirty_background_bytes question, not a nixstore
one, and it is the next thing to test in the box.

- DISCARDED: drop a deduped file's dirty pages via ftruncate through a
  descriptor borrowed from the restore. Mechanism was sound and it
  fired exactly where intended (8 dup + 8 ftruncate on a tree with 8
  blobs over the 1 MiB threshold), but it measured null twice. Host:
  device sectors 3-39 MiB either way under a 512 MiB cap, noise. In the
  box, on the real shape (linux-firmware + linux-headers + sway, /usr =
  1805 MiB), the all-dup re-commit wrote 142 MiB with it off and 176
  MiB with it on. The reason is that of 1812 MiB deduped only ~8%
  reaches the disk at all: the hasher dedups each file within
  milliseconds of it being written, so the unlink already drops the
  pages before writeback reaches them, and ftruncate has nothing left
  to discard. Removed, along with the CreateRegularFileSink::
  dupContentsFd virtual it needed.

