## the stutter in real time (bench/trace-stall.py)

The counter and the write path on one clock: strace -ttt gives absolute
epoch timestamps, -T the duration of each call, and the counter paints
are timestamped off a pty. 3.3 GiB driver tree, same binary both runs.

  unconstrained  4.21 s, 46,517 files, 84,119 writes
                 counter frozen >= 0.25s: never
                 17% of the import inside write()

  MEMMAX=1G     10.31 s, 46,725 files, 84,122 writes
                 counter frozen >= 0.25s: twice, 4.56 s, 44% of import
                 59% of the import inside write()

    freeze  files  writes  in write()  longest
     4.21s      1    1542       4.13s    2.73s
     0.35s     32     940       0.19s    0.01s

The first line is the whole answer. During a 4.21 second freeze the
counter advanced by **one file**, and 4.13 of those 4.21 seconds were
spent inside write() -- 98% of the freeze, one call of which took 2.73
seconds on its own. Same write count as the unthrottled run, so this is
not extra work; it is the same work held still.

The chain, end to end: the restore thread is the only thread that
writes (write() time by thread: 6.05 s on one tid, 0.00 s on every
other), so when balance_dirty_pages parks it, it pushes no events to
AsyncFileHasher; the hasher has no file to finish, so end() never runs,
so progressTick never fires. The frozen number is a direct readout of a
thread sitting in the kernel. Nothing in the import is slow.

Second, independent source of the same feeling, and this one is pure
presentation: progressTick fires once per completed *file*. A 200 MB
blob shows no motion at all while it is being written, even on a
perfectly healthy import. On a driver generation that is several
seconds of visible stillness that has nothing to do with throttling.
Ticking on bytes as well as files would fix that half without
pretending to fix the I/O.

- DISCARDED: self-pacing writeback. Arm sync_file_range write-behind
  only after observing a write() slower than 50 ms, so it stays dormant
  where there is page cache headroom (measured: max write 0.000 s
  unconstrained, never armed) and engages only under pressure, keeping
  two 8 MiB windows in flight instead of the whole file. It did what it
  claimed -- time inside write() 10.69 -> 7.18 s, longest single write
  3.22 -> 1.92 s -- and was still a loss: import 17.0 -> 19.8 s, and on
  the counter-gap metric 9.65/12.43 s (off) against 15.15/15.87 s (on),
  two runs each. The waits it adds are not free, they are just spent in
  sync_file_range instead of write, and there are more of them.
  Third attempt in this family after unconditional write-behind and the
  ftruncate page-drop, all measured, all negative. The pattern is worth
  stating once: the bytes have to reach the disk, and rearranging when
  the writer blocks does not make the disk faster. On a box with no page
  cache headroom the writer waits either way.

## the shell side of a commit, attributed (bench/vm-shell.sh)

In the box, driver generation, 79,023 entries, /usr = 1805 MiB:

  savemeta total     1011 ms
    walk (one find)    52
    two sorts           9
    getcap            122
    getfacl           186
    getfattr          143
    lsattr -d         342
    (remainder ~157 ms: the tab/newline scan, the perms manifest, the
     manifest writes)

Traversal is 5% of it. The per-path probes are 793 ms, 78%, and
lsattr -d alone is a third of savemeta because it opens every inode.
That is the correction to the walk-merging result above: collapsing six
walks to three was right and its ceiling was always low, because the
walking was never the cost.

Why any of this is outside the store at all, since it keeps coming up:
NAR keeps a node's type, its contents and the executable bit and
nothing else. No mode, no uid/gid, no xattrs, no ACLs, no capabilities,
no chattr flags. That is deliberate and load-bearing: it is what makes
two trees differing only in ownership hash to the same store path,
which is what makes dedup work. A bootable rootfs still needs
/etc/shadow at 0600 and setcap binaries with their capabilities, so
savemeta captures that as ordinary files inside the tree, where it
becomes content: hashed, deduped, rolled back with the generation. It
has to run before the dump, so it cannot ride the import's own walk.

- DISCARDED (unsafe, not slow): probe the filesystem once for xattr and
  chattr-flag support and skip those two walks when it says no. Would
  have removed 485 ms of the 1011, and every one of those calls really
  does return EOPNOTSUPP on a box whose lower is squashfs and whose
  upper is tmpfs. The safety argument was that -xdev means one
  filesystem, so one probe on the root settles the tree. That is false
  here: -xdev sees one st_dev, but on a booted generation that device
  is overlayfs over two filesystems with different capabilities, and a
  probe landing on the tmpfs upper would skip flags that files still on
  the ext4 lower genuinely carry. Silent metadata loss, of exactly the
  class arch/tests/meta-test.sh was written for ("this happened: user
  .bashrc came back 0444 root:root") -- and that test runs on plain
  ext4, so it would not have caught it. Reverted. A safe version needs
  a probe that is honest about the union, not about st_dev.

- PROMOTED (free): progressTick takes a byte count beside the item
  count. The import counter advances once per completed file, so a
  single large blob left it motionless for as long as the file took to
  write -- seconds on a driver generation, on a completely healthy
  import, with no throttling involved. Bytes move throughout, so the
  pair separates "working on something big" from "not working", which
  the file count alone cannot. Demonstrated on an 800 MiB single-file
  tree: the count holds at 50 while the byte figure climbs 185 -> 375
  -> 566 -> 756 MiB, where before the line read "importing: 50" for the
  whole file. Called once per data chunk (~84k times on a 3.3 GiB
  tree); the existing 80 ms repaint throttle and the cached isTTY()
  check keep that to a lock and a clock read, and off a terminal to a
  bool. Both figures still only grow, so repaints never need to erase.
